@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Exploring .NET Project Types";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "DotNetCore";
    ViewData["ActivePage"] = "ProjectTypes";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">
        .NET lets you create many types of applications. Each project type serves a specific purpose. Let’s explore the most common ones you’ll use as a beginner.
    </p>
    <hr />

    <!-- Section: Overview -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Common .NET Project Types</h2>
        <p>When creating a new project in Visual Studio, you’ll see a wide range of templates. Here are the key ones:</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Console App -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>1. Console Application</h3>
        <p>
            A basic app that runs in the command line. Perfect for learning and small utilities.
        </p>

        <div class="code-snippet bg-light p-3 rounded position-relative">
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>// Console App Example
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Welcome to .NET!");
    }
}</code></pre>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: ASP.NET Core Web App -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>2. ASP.NET Core Web Application</h3>
        <p>
            Build web apps and APIs using modern tools and MVC architecture. ASP.NET Core is cross-platform, fast, and widely used in enterprise development.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Web UI with Razor Pages or MVC</li>
            <li>REST APIs with controllers</li>
            <li>Blazor for C# in the browser</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Class Library -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>3. Class Library</h3>
        <p>
            Used to create reusable code (DLLs) that can be shared across multiple projects — great for writing helper functions, services, or models.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: .NET MAUI App -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>4. .NET MAUI App (Cross-Platform)</h3>
        <p>
            Build native apps for Android, iOS, Windows, and macOS using a single C# codebase and XAML for UI. Ideal for mobile and desktop applications.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Test Project -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>5. Unit Test Project</h3>
        <p>
            A project template used to test your application logic using frameworks like MSTest, xUnit, or NUnit.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <p>
            Choosing the right project type is the first step in building a successful application. Start with a Console App to learn the basics, then explore Web, MAUI, or Class Libraries based on your goals.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Navigation Buttons -->
    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("InstallVS", "DotNet")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">
            &larr; Previous: Installing Visual Studio
        </a>
        <a href="@Url.Action("HelloWorld", "DotNet")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">
            Next: First .NET Project – Hello World &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>
</div>

<script>
    document.querySelectorAll('.copy-btn').forEach(btn => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
            const codeText = this.parentElement.querySelector('code')?.innerText;
            if (codeText) {
                navigator.clipboard.writeText(codeText).then(() => {
                    alert('Code copied to clipboard!');
                });
            }
        });
    });
</script>
